course: dictionary
lesson: 18
title: Abreast: side by side, and up to date
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Abreast is a word about position. When people or things go abreast, they stand or move side by side, all facing the same way. «They start taking turns, and on wide bits they walk three abreast like cowboys in a film.» The clue is three: three people cannot walk in a straight line side by side, so they walk next to each other.
explain: Abreast of something means level with it. «The car drew abreast of the bus.» Picture it: the car has moved forward until it is beside the bus. Try the swap test: «The car drew beside the bus» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: The word also appears in the phrase keep abreast of. To keep abreast of news means to stay up to date with it, like moving alongside the news so you do not fall behind. This phrase is more formal, so you will often find it in serious writing and speech.
fact: Abreast is an adverb, and it describes how people or things are arranged or moving.
fact: «Three abreast» means three side by side, all facing the same way.
fact: «Draw abreast» means move forward until you are level with something.
fact: «Keep abreast of» means stay informed about news or changes.
fact: Road safety rules say when cyclists may ride two abreast, so always check the rules where you live.
exercise: «They walk three abreast down the lane.» What does abreast mean here? | Very quickly | Behind each other in a line | Lost and far apart | *Side by side
exercise: «The car drew abreast of the bus.» What does drew abreast of mean? | Sped away from the bus | Stopped far behind the bus | *Came level with the bus | Hit the bus from behind
exercise: Mum says, «Keep abreast of the news.» What does she mean? | *Stay up to date with the news | Shout the news to the neighbours | Read the news out loud in a loud voice | Forget the news straight away
test: «Two girls ran abreast across the field.» Which word tells you the girls were side by side? | Field | Across | *Abreast | Ran
test: Ben reads, «She tries to keep abreast of changes at work.» What does keep abreast of mean? | *To stay up to date with | To copy | To argue with | To leave work early
exam: «The cyclists rode two abreast, then one behind the other.» What does two abreast mean? | Riding in a single line | Riding very fast | Riding in the dark | *Riding side by side
